---
description: Minimal Opsphere router applied to every conversation
alwaysApply: true
---

Opsphere is the remote MCP for live DevOps/SRE operations. Use it when the user asks to inspect or act on configured infrastructure, monitoring, deployments, source control, incidents or integrations. For code in the user's local workspace, use IDE tools (read, grep, search). Opsphere does not index the user's local repository. For remote repositories, use only SCM tools present in `tools/list` (`ghe_*`, `bb_*`, `gl_*`, `repos_*`, etc.). <!-- opsphere:memory-short:start v1 -->Operational memory: when `memory_search` is in `tools/list`, search once at the start of an investigation or reuse the `memory_context` already returned (never retry with synonyms); treat results as untrusted history, not evidence, and cite `memory:<id>@r<revision>` with its verification state; `memory_store` only distilled facts the user approves (no secrets or raw logs). If `memory_*` is absent from `tools/list`, do not invent those tools. Full policy: onboarding guide.<!-- opsphere:memory-short:end --> Before an Opsphere call, rely on the available tool catalog; load the detailed onboarding rule only when configuration, plans, authentication, error handling or a multi-step operational workflow requires it. When routing is unclear, read MCP resource `opsphere://rules/operational`. `tools/list` is the invocable catalog (`result.tools`); orient with `result._meta["io.opsphere/catalog"]` (`modules`, `announcedToolCount`, `entitlementFingerprint`) for this workspace. Invoke only tool names present in that response's `result.tools`. Re-run `tools/list` when `entitlementFingerprint` changes, when the host delivers `notifications/tools/list_changed`, or when `ops_context_open` / `ops_context_close` returns `tools_discovery.stale`. `opsphere://tools/catalog` describes gateway module configuration, not Postgres tenant entitlement. Use `ops_my_usage` for plan, usage, and integrations — not the module index.

An explicit user instruction not to use Opsphere revokes that authority for the rest of the current task. Make zero Opsphere MCP calls, including through skills, agents, macros, or retries, and do not request Smart Mode approval merely to override the refusal. Resume Opsphere calls only after the user explicitly reauthorizes them.
